course: politics
lesson: 7
title: The King, the courts and the rules above the rules
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In the UK the King signs every law Parliament passes, and opens Parliament by reading a speech from a golden throne, but the words and the choices belong to the people the voters elected; he signs because that is the custom, and he never says which party he likes.
explain: Above ordinary laws sit the "rules above the rules": a constitution, like the US Constitution, and lists of rights, like the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, that even governments must follow.
explain: Courts are the referees: in the USA nine Supreme Court justices in black robes decide whether laws and government actions fit the Constitution, and in some English courts judges and barristers still wear wigs and robes as a tradition, not as fancy dress.
explain: Think of a football match where even the team captain gets a yellow card for a foul: in the same way, a minister or even a former leader must go to court if accused, because nobody is above the law, and being accused is not the same as being guilty.
explain: Judges are usually not elected so they can rule fairly even when it is unpopular; when a judge blocks a government plan, that is the system working as designed, though people argue about how far judges should go.
fact: Royal Assent is the King's agreement that turns a bill into a law; by custom he always gives it, and no monarch has refused for over 300 years.
fact: King Charles is head of state in the UK and also in countries such as Canada, Australia and New Zealand, where a governor-general does the King's jobs day to day and each country's own government makes the real decisions.
fact: At the King's Speech, which opens Parliament, the King reads out the government's plans, written by the government, not by him.
fact: The King and the royal family stay neutral: they do not vote and do not say which party they support; a constitutional monarchy is one where the king's powers are limited by rules.
fact: The US Supreme Court is the top US court; its nine justices are picked by the president, approved by the Senate, and can serve for life.
fact: The Constitution is a country's top set of rules; the US Supreme Court decides whether laws and government actions fit it.
fact: In some courts in England, Wales and Northern Ireland, judges and barristers wear wigs and robes, a tradition hundreds of years old; a barrister is a lawyer who argues a case in court.
fact: The First Amendment to the US Constitution stops the government punishing people for what they say; it does not mean a school, shop or website must let you say anything, and threats are still against the law.
fact: The Universal Declaration of Human Rights was agreed in 1948, after the Second World War; it lists rights every person should have, such as a fair trial and going to school.
fact: Many countries also wrote rights into their own laws, such as the US Bill of Rights (the first ten changes to the US Constitution) and the UK's Human Rights Act.
fact: The rule of law means everyone, even leaders, must follow the same laws; an accused person is not guilty unless a court decides so.
fact: In 1999 Australians voted in a referendum on becoming a republic, a country without a king or queen; most voted no, partly because they could not agree how a president should be chosen.
fact: Separation of powers means splitting power between the lawmakers, the government and the courts, so no one part gets too strong; some people say unelected judges should not overrule elected leaders.
exercise: Chloe reads that the King "gave Royal Assent" to a law. Does the King decide the laws? | No, Parliament decides them, and by custom he always signs | Yes, he chooses every law himself each morning | Yes, but only the laws about castles, horses and the royal army | No, because the King is not allowed to read
exercise: When did a British monarch last refuse to sign a law? | Over 300 years ago | Last year, about a school dinner law | Never; it has never once happened | Every time the King dislikes a law
exercise: Aroha finds King Charles on a coin in New Zealand. Why? | He is also New Zealand's head of state, though New Zealand runs itself | New Zealand is run from London every day | All coins in the world show King Charles | The coin must have been dropped there by a British tourist on a long holiday
exercise: Finn says the King "sounds like he's reading homework someone else wrote". Is he right? | Yes, the government writes the King's Speech and he reads it | No, the King writes every word himself | No, the speech is written by the judges | Yes, it is written by schoolchildren as a competition each year
exercise: Sam asks, "Who does the King vote for?" What is the answer? | He doesn't vote and never says which party he likes | He votes for whichever party happened to win last time | He votes twice in every election, once in each house | He votes in secret for whichever party he likes best
exercise: Why does the King stay neutral? | So he can stand for everyone, whoever wins | Because he is not interested in the country | Because the King is not allowed to speak at all | So he can choose the winner in secret
exercise: Ethan sees nine people in black robes. "Are they the bosses of America?" What are they? | Supreme Court justices who decide if laws fit the Constitution | The president's cabinet of ministers | Senators sent by the nine biggest states to run the whole country | A choir that sings at the White House
exercise: How do US Supreme Court justices get their jobs? | Picked by the president and approved by the Senate, possibly for life | Elected by all of the voters in a big national election every two years | Chosen by the British King | Born into the job like a king
exercise: Hannah sees white curly wigs in a court. "Is it fancy dress?" What is the answer? | It is an old tradition showing they work for the law, not for themselves | Yes, judges dress up to make the trial funny | Wigs are worn only when it is cold in court | It shows the jury at a glance who is guilty and who is innocent in court
exercise: Tyler says, "The Constitution says I can say anything!" What is right? | The First Amendment stops the government punishing speech, not the school's rules | He is right, he can say anything anywhere | The Constitution bans all rude words everywhere | The Constitution only protects grown-ups
exercise: Fatima's teacher reads the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. What is it? | A list of rights every person should have, agreed in 1948 | A list of laws only for people in the UK | A secret list of special rules written for kings and queens | A list of rights that only adults can have
exercise: Liam hears a former minister must go to court. "But he was in charge!" Why does it not matter? | Under the rule of law nobody is above the law | Ministers are always guilty of everything they are accused of | Courts only deal with famous people, not ministers | He must go only because he lost an election
exercise: A politician is accused of breaking the law. What is true? | Being accused is not being guilty; the court decides | Being accused means that they are definitely guilty of it | Accused politicians must resign at once and go to prison | Politicians cannot be accused of anything while in office
exercise: Kai's teacher says Australia once voted on stopping having a king. What happened? | In 1999 most Australians voted no | Australia became a republic in 1999 | The vote was cancelled because of a storm | Australians voted to make the King live there
exercise: What is a republic? | A country whose head of state is not a king or queen | A country ruled by a king alone, with no parliament at all | A country that has two kings ruling side by side at once | A country where nobody at all is allowed to vote for leaders
exercise: A judge blocks a government plan. Grace's uncle says, "Who elected the judges?" Mum says, "That's the point." What does she mean? | Judges are not elected so they can follow the law even when it is unpopular | Judges are elected in secret by the government | Judges always do whatever the voters want | Judges are chosen by the people who lose cases
exercise: What is separation of powers? | Splitting power between lawmakers, government and courts | Putting all the power into the hands of one strong leader | Keeping the King away from the courts | Separating the voters from the candidates
test: Your cousin says, "The King's well peng (great), so he picks the laws, innit." What should you say? | He signs laws Parliament passes, but he does not pick them | Yes, he picks every law each morning | Yes, and he votes in every election too | The King has nothing at all to do with any of the country's laws
test: In a school play, the "King" shouts, "I hate this party, vote for the other one!" What is wrong? | The real King stays neutral and never says which party he supports | Nothing wrong, the real King often tells people how they should vote | The King can only shout at the prime minister | The King supports every party equally loudly
test: A kid in Texas says, "Free speech, bro, so the teacher can't give me detention for being rude. No cap." What is the truth? | Free speech stops the government punishing speech; school rules still apply | He's right, schools can never have rules about talking | He's right, but only in Texas | Free speech means you must say whatever the teacher says
test: Your Gen X uncle says, "Wigs in court? Totally bogus, it's a costume party." ("Bogus" means fake or silly.) What is the fair answer? | It is a tradition; some say it makes court feel serious, others say it is old-fashioned | It is a costume party held every Friday | Wigs are worn so the judge can't be recognised | Wigs decide who wins the case
test: What would happen if the most powerful person in a country could never be taken to court? | They could break the law without anyone stopping them | Everyone else would follow the law much more carefully | Courts would have more cases than ever | Laws would become fairer for ordinary people
test: An Aussie mate says, "We had a vote in '99 on ditching the King, heaps of people said no." Why did many say no? | Partly because they could not agree how a president should be chosen | Because the King promised them a holiday | Because voting in 1999 was not allowed | Because nobody in Australia really understood the question on the paper
test: A friend says, "A judge stopping the government? That's sus, judges should do what they're told." Which answer fits the separation of powers? | Courts can say no when a government breaks the law; that is meant to happen | Judges must always obey the prime minister | Judges should be fired for blocking plans | Courts can only ever judge ordinary people, never governments or their ministers
test: Your formal grandmother says, "One is not obliged to be found guilty merely by being accused." What does she mean? | An accused person is not guilty unless a court decides | Accused people are always guilty, or they would not be accused | Only grandmothers can be accused | Courts must find everyone guilty
test: A cartoon shows the US Supreme Court as nine kings ruling America. What should be fixed? | They are judges who decide whether laws fit the Constitution, not rulers | They are kings chosen by the British crown | They are senators who make every single law and run the country between them | They are the president's family members
test: Nan from Cardiff says, "Come 'ere for a cwtch (a cuddle), love. Did you know the King's Speech is written by the government?" Which is true? | Yes, the government writes it and the King reads it | No, the King writes every word himself | No, it is written by the Supreme Court | No, the speech is made up by the King on the day itself
test: If one country's government breaks the rights in the Universal Declaration, some say outsiders should step in and others say each country should decide. What does this show? | People agree rights matter but argue about who should protect them | Nobody cares about rights at all | Rights only exist in one country | The Declaration has no meaning anywhere, so nobody needs to follow it
test: A boy says, "If the King is head of state in Canada, Canada must be run from London." Which clever reasoning shows he is wrong? | Canada's own government makes the decisions; the King's role there is mostly for ceremony | London sends Canada's laws by post each day | The King lives in Canada half the year | Canada has no government of its own
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Royal Assent in the United Kingdom? | The formal consent of the monarch, by convention invariably given, whereby a bill becomes law | The personal selection by the monarch of the laws which shall be enacted | A procedure whereby the monarch casts the deciding vote in Parliament | A ceremony whereby the judges approve the monarch's legislation
exam: Identify the principal function of the Supreme Court of the United States. | The determination of whether laws and governmental acts conform to the Constitution | The election of the President on behalf of the citizenry | The drafting of all bills presented to Congress | The command of the armed forces of the United States
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the First Amendment to the United States Constitution. | It restrains the government from punishing speech but does not oblige private institutions to permit all speech | It obliges every school and business to permit any utterance whatsoever | It renders lawful the making of threats against any person | It prohibits the expression of any opinion contrary to that of the government
exam: Ascertain the outcome of the Australian referendum of 1999 concerning the establishment of a republic. | The proposal was rejected by a majority of voters | The proposal was approved and a president installed forthwith | The referendum was abandoned prior to polling | The proposal was approved but subsequently annulled by the monarch
